# Foundation Document, Gettysburg National Military Park (National Park Service; August 2016) and two nps.gov pages (Devil's Den rehabilitation, the July 2 and 3 United States line)
# A summary document of 2016: its figures are the park's own of 2016 (modern states); its statement that more than 40 farmsteads with structures of the battle lie within the park is a count of surviving buildings. Web pages describe the park of the 2020s.
@ NPS-FD-2016

7/3 | fields | The park of 2016: woodlots and woodlands 1,258 acres, orchards 160 acres, 80 to 100 miles of fencing and walls | - | ~2016 (the park's figures of 2016: a mostly rehabilitated and agricultural state, not 1863) | stated | The document says the majority of parklands are agricultural or pastoral, with woodlots and woodlands accounting for 1,258 acres, 160 acres of orchards and 80 to 100 miles of fencing and walls | #s18
12/8 | buildings | Over 40 farmstead properties with structures that were present during the 1863 battle; many were headquarters, fortifications and field hospitals | - | 1863-07 (farmsteads whose structures stood in 1863; the count of those within the park in 2016) | stated | The document says the park includes over 40 farmstead properties with structures that were present during the 1863 battle, that the nineteenth-century agricultural buildings range from farmhouses and barns to summer kitchens, wagon sheds, smokehouses, woodsheds, corn cribs, chicken coops and privies, and that many of the surviving buildings served as headquarters, fortifications and field hospitals on July 1-3, 1863 | #s2-4
10/6 | general | The agrarian landscape was used militarily mostly for defense; hills and wooded areas affected maneuvers; railroads, roads and farm lanes were avenues of approach | - | 1863-07 | stated | The document says the topographic and landscape features of the 1863 battlefield significantly influenced the battle, that features of the agrarian landscape were employed militarily mostly for defensive purposes while hills and wooded areas affected maneuvers and troop movements, and that historic railroads, roads and farm lanes offered avenues of approach | #s8-10
10/6 | monuments | Most monuments and commemorative features were built between 1880 and 1927; Union volunteer regiments' monuments mainly in the Memorial Association era (1863-1895) | - | !1863-1927 (commemorative features after the battle) | stated | The document says that most monuments and other commemorative features were constructed between 1880 and 1927 and that during the Gettysburg Battlefield Memorial Association era (1863-1895) monuments to Union volunteer regiments were mainly built | #s16-17

@ NPSGOV-DEVILS-DEN-REHAB

1/- | woodlots | Devil's Den rehabilitation: removal of woody vegetation to better reflect 1863 conditions | - | ~2020s (a rehabilitation project; the woody growth removed is later than 1863) | stated | The page says the primary goal of this phase of the project is to remove vegetation to better reflect 1863 conditions, Stage 1 removing woody species from the rocky outcroppings and the upper trail portion of Devil's Den | #s27 | #s29

@ NPSGOV-US-LINE

1/- | general | Most of the United States line of July 2 and 3 followed Cemetery Ridge, a mostly open and exposed ridgeline south of Gettysburg | - | ~2020s (the page's present-tense description of the ridge) | stated | The page says most of the United States line followed Cemetery Ridge, a mostly open and exposed ridgeline that extends south from Gettysburg | #s5
